WEBVTT


00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.950
Working with cones of yarn that are
slippery presents some challenges.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.340
The yarn can slip down into a pool on
the floor and then catch beneath the cone

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:17.640
caught like this, it can also break and
jam and snag your knitting and cause all

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.510
sorts of frustration when knitting.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:26.840
This yarn can slip out of the hooks and
jump up and a loop over the gate pegs.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:34.445
Here are some tips for working with
slippery yarn to help keep the yarn on the

00:00:34.445 --> 00:00:40.114
cone, place it in a plastic bag, and
scrunch that bag down so the plastic is

00:00:40.144 --> 00:00:41.855
bunched up around the bottom.

00:00:42.665 --> 00:00:47.435
This will help keep the yarn from
pooling and catching underneath the cone.

00:00:48.785 --> 00:00:50.375
You can use yarn bras.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.280
Nylon stockings or even compression
bandages to keep the slippery yarn in

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:56.820
place.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.710
Just remember that anything that
constricts the yarn coming off the cone

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.790
can impact your gauge.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.050
So swatch using the same method.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:14.070
If you find your slippery yarn or any yarn
for that matter, if it tends to jump off

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:19.860
the needles and mis-forms stitches or
it gets caught around the gate pegs, you

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.040
might want to try adding
more weight to your knitting.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:28.380
The key here is to use even
weight across the work.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.740
Try using a ribber cast on comb.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:38.710
Using waste yarn, pull out every
other needle and knit across.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.280
Now insert the iubber
cast on comb in the loops.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:51.740
You'll see I've pushed that cast on comb
back so the teeth come out in front of the

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:56.479
gate pegs and I can slip the wire through.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.220
You'll also notice that I've marked the
center tooth of the comb with a bright

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:03.180
nail Polish.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:07.860
This makes it easier to align that
comb to this center of the work.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.990
Again, our goal here is to add even
weight all the way across the work.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:21.050
After you've inserted the wire, you
carefully just knit one row and pull down

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:22.550
on that cast on comb.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:27.950
Put all your needles into work and
continue to knit a few rows with waste

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:28.290
yarn.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:36.330
Hang some weights on the cast on comb, and
you might need to experiment a little bit,

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.890
not too much weight, but enough weight
so the stitches will form properly.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.030
Cast on with your main
yarn and knit your garment.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.700
You'll find with this method that the
stitches are going to behave a little

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.950
better, even with slippery yarn.

00:02:53.385 --> 00:02:58.425
And don't forget for accurate gauge
measurement, you need to swatch using this

00:02:58.425 --> 00:02:59.445
method as well.

00:03:00.525 --> 00:03:03.015
Don't let slippery yarn
get the best of you.

00:03:07.195 --> 00:03:08.545
Thank you for watching.

00:03:08.815 --> 00:03:14.035
